# GitLab CODEOWNERS for automation-registry. Replace the @group names with your real groups.
# Combine with Settings > Merge requests > Approval rules > "Code owners" (required) so these approvals are enforced.

# Default: the platform team reviews everything.
*                                   @automation-platform

# Registry code, CI and the schema change the governance gate itself -> platform maintainers only (2 approvals recommended).
[Registry tooling and CI][2]
/registry/                          @automation-platform/maintainers
/tests/                             @automation-platform/maintainers
/schema/                            @automation-platform/maintainers
/.gitlab-ci.yml                     @automation-platform/maintainers
/CODEOWNERS                         @automation-platform/maintainers

# The manifest: platform approves ring promotion / fleet blocks; automation owners approve their own stanza (see below).
[Manifest and history]
/manifest.yaml                      @automation-platform
/history/                           @automation-platform
/portal.config.json                 @automation-platform

# Governance document is owned by risk/security.
[Governance]
/docs/GOVERNANCE.md                 @security-governance @automation-platform

# GitLab CODEOWNERS works per file, not per YAML block, so an automation owner cannot be a *required* approver for just their stanza.
# The ops-action MR description carries a review checklist and names the requester; ask the owner (see `owner` in the manifest) to
# approve explicitly. If you need hard enforcement, split the manifest per automation (manifest.d/<name>.yaml merged at build time).
